   107	    ruling 1 on the slice-3 design and applies the slice-2 narrow-form ruling
   108	    in its own words: the contract root "covers what the contract asserts, no
   109	    more". Rollback, fork, rewrite and replacement are conditions
   110	    ``witness-protocol.html`` names, about a layer that sits *above* the
   111	    backend contract and compares two heads it obtained through it —
   112	    ``backend-contract.html`` does not name one of them. Joining the contract
   113	    root would assert that every conforming backend owes these refusals, and
   114	    only a contract-text line can make that claim.
   115	    """
   116	
   117	
   118	class HeadRollback(AttestationRefusal):
   119	    """The presented head sits below the head this machine last accepted.
   120	
   121	    §11's response is to locate a replica or backup at or above the witnessed
   122	    head. Never move the witness backward to match the store: that is the
   123	    silent re-acceptance the journal exists to prevent, and the only licensed
   124	    way down is the explicit trust-reset ceremony (:attr:`Kind.TRUST_RESET`).
   125	    """
   126	
   127	
   128	class HeadFork(AttestationRefusal):
   129	    """Two different records claim the same ordinal in the same lineage.
   130	
   131	    Named ``HeadFork`` rather than ``SameHeightFork`` because
   132	    ``arrival_contract.SameHeightFork`` already exists and means something
   133	    adjacent but different — replication found a conflicting record at the
   134	    height it was about to fill. This is a comparison of two heads. Same
   135	    ratified *state* name (the outcome string stays ``same-height-fork``),
   136	    different operation, different type: the state machine's vocabulary is
   137	    preserved where it is normative and the Python names stay unambiguous
   138	    where a collision would bite.
   139	
   140	    §11's response is to freeze both branches and collect evidence. Never pick
   141	    the taller branch — height is not evidence of continuity.
   142	    """
   143	
   144	
   145	class HeadRewrite(AttestationRefusal):
   146	    """The head advanced, but the record at the known ordinal is not the known one.
   147	
   148	    The chain in front of us may verify perfectly and still be a different
   149	    history than the one we accepted. Checking only that a walk reached the
   150	    presented head answers nothing about continuity with what came before,
   151	    which is precisely the "never accept height as proof of continuity"
   152	    failure.
   153	    """
   154	
   155	
   156	class LineageReplaced(AttestationRefusal):
   157	    """A different lineage is presented where a known one was expected.
   158	
   159	    Where the expected lineage is declared, the backend's own
   160	    ``NotAuthority`` fires first and is already correct. This exists because
   161	    the classifier owns the row the vectors exercise, and because a caller
   162	    that reached the comparison without a declared lineage still needs it.
   163	    """
   164	
   165	
   166	class StoreLost(AttestationRefusal):
   167	    """The log is gone, but the journal remembers a head for it.
   168	
   169	    The attack matrix's "delete the store and every backup" row, where the
   170	    witness proves what was *lost* rather than its contents. It must refuse,
   171	    and in particular it must never permit a silent re-mint under a remembered
   172	    lineage: a fresh genesis at a location whose journal remembers ordinal
   173	    4217 is a replacement wearing the old name. Recovery is the explicit
   174	    trust-reset ceremony.
   175	    """
   176	
   177	
   178	class JournalEquivocation(AttestationRefusal):
   179	    """The journal itself holds two incompatible histories.
   180	
   181	    Two entries at the maximum ordinal of the current trust epoch with
   182	    different record hashes. The journal has recorded both and it is not the
   183	    journal's place to choose between them.
   184	    """
   185	
   186	
   187	class JournalUnreadable(AttestationRefusal):
   188	    """There is nothing to parse: the journal cannot be read as text at all.
   189	
   190	    A directory where the journal should be, a permission wall, bytes that
   191	    are not text. Distinct from an *absent* journal, which is first contact,
   192	    and from a journal whose *content* cannot be read, which is
   193	    :class:`HeadUnreadable`. Raised rather than let out as the underlying
   194	    ``OSError`` so that a caller catching :class:`AttestationRefusal` does not
   195	    have a builtin escape past it.
   196	
   197	    **This is the only thing that raises it, and the scope has narrowed
   198	    twice.** It once covered damage above the last line, then entries with no
   199	    header. Both are now tolerated: an unreadable line is skipped and
   200	    reported, a missing header is reported as an absence, and in each case the
   201	    loss is carried in the result type (:class:`HeadLowerBound`) instead of
   202	    converted into a verdict. Refusing made an ordinary crash a permanent
   203	    incident, and it claimed a protection this location cannot deliver anyway
   204	    — anyone able to corrupt the journal can delete it instead and be met with
   205	    trust-on-first-use. Where a weakened claim is available, a refusal is a
   206	    verdict the evidence does not support.
   207	    """
   208	
   209	
   210	class IndeterminateComparison(AttestationRefusal):
   211	    """A full comparison was asked of a journal whose read was incomplete.
   212	
   213	    See :class:`HeadLowerBound` for what "incomplete" means and why the term
   214	    is not "unreadable".
   215	
   216	    Not a claim about the store. The journal's own record of what this machine
   217	    accepted has a hole in it, so "unchanged" is a question this journal can no
   218	    longer answer — while "rollback" is one it still can, for anything below
   219	    the bound. Raised by :meth:`JournalRead.established_head` so the cheap
   220	    unchanged shortcut cannot be taken by a caller who simply forgot to look.
   221	    """
   222	
   223	
   224	class UnsafeLineageName(AttestationRefusal):
   225	    """This lineage cannot be used as a journal filename.
   226	
   227	    ``lin`` is validated only as a non-empty string on the wire, and while
   228	    ``mint_lineage()`` produces a ULID, a foreign or hostile genesis need not.
   229	    Scope-the-claim: this is a location claim ("this cache cannot key on that
   230	    string"), NOT a verdict about the store — refusing to resolve a path says
   231	    nothing about whether the store is trustworthy.
   232	    """
   233	
   234	
   235	# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   236	# The observation record (§B.2)
   237	# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   238	
   239	
   240	class Kind(Enum):
   241	    """Why this entry exists."""
   242	
   243	    BOOTSTRAP = "bootstrap"  # the first entry for a lineage
   244	    ADVANCE = "advance"  # the head moved
   245	    AUDIT = "audit"  # a full chain-plus-projection audit ran
   246	    TRUST_RESET = "trust-reset"  # an operator ceremony re-established trust
   247	
   248	
   249	class Level(Enum):
   250	    """What evidence backed this entry's head.
   251	
   252	    The one addition to §04's field table, and it is the scope-the-claim
   253	    field: it states how much was actually established, so an entry can never
   254	    be read as a stronger claim than the evidence behind it.
   255	    """
   256	
   257	    MINT = "mint"  # this process minted the genesis
   258	    FIRST_CONTACT = "first-contact"  # trust on first use; nothing was proven
   259	    COMMIT = "commit"  # appended by this process under the full-head fence
   260	    DESCENDANT = "descendant"  # a verified suffix walk from the previous head
   261	    FULL = "full"  # verified from genesis
   262	
   263	
   264	@dataclass(frozen=True)
   265	class HeadAttestation:
   266	    """One observation of a lineage's head, at a moment, with its evidence.
   267	
   268	    Field choices, against §04's table:
   269	
   270	    * **The whole** :class:`~engine.arrival_contract.Head`, **always.** §04's
   271	      lineage/ordinal/record_hash are exactly that type, and reusing it rather
   272	      than restating three fields means this comparison cannot drift from the
   273	      backend's own notion of a head.
   274	    * **Counts omitted** — projection corroboration smuggled into a custody
   275	      claim. Absent from the type, not optional-and-unset.
   276	    * **Signed fields absent**, per the module docstring.
   277	    * **protocol/wire omitted from the record**, carried by the file header:
   278	      they are properties of the journal, not of each entry, and a field
   279	      nothing reads is how speculative grammar ossifies.
   280	    * **observed_at kept.** It is what makes a refusal actionable ("your
   281	      remembered head is from three weeks ago") and what answers audit
   282	      staleness. Witness metadata, never ledger order.
   283	
   284	    ``location`` is diagnosis only and is **never** a key: journals are keyed
   285	    by lineage so that a worktree, a move, or a copy still finds the lineage's
   286	    memory. ``note`` carries the trust-reset gap description and the audit
   287	    result.
   288	    """
   289	
   290	    head: Head
   291	    kind: Kind
   292	    level: Level
   293	    observed_at: float
   294	    location: str = ""
   295	    note: str = ""
   296	    follows: tuple[int, str] | None = None
   297	    """The PHYSICAL LINE and identity of the entry this one was appended after
   298	    — REQUIRED on a trust reset, ``None`` on every other kind.
   299	
   300	    ``(0, "")`` is the honest claim for a reset that opens an empty journal:
   301	    line numbers start at 1, so zero says "there was no predecessor" rather
   302	    than "no claim was made", which is what ``None`` says and what makes an
   303	    unbound reset invalid.
   304	
   305	    **Not the deferred** ``previous``. That one is the signed grammar's
   306	    per-entry chain link over the whole journal, and it stays absent: this is
   307	    unsigned, it is carried by exactly one kind, and it makes exactly one
   308	    claim — where this reset sat, and what sat in front of it.
   309	
   310	    It exists because a trust reset was REPLAYABLE, and it took two rounds to
   311	    get right. An entry is just bytes in an append-only file, so re-appending
   312	    an old reset re-opened the epoch it had closed
   313	    (``finding:s3wp3-trust-reset-replay``). Binding the reset to its
   314	    predecessor's IDENTITY closed the one-line replay — and sol r2 defeated
   315	    that too, by replaying the historical predecessor AND the reset as an
   316	    ordered suffix, which reproduces the recorded identity exactly.
   317	
   318	    **The position is what closes the class rather than the instance.** Copied
   319	    bytes appended later always land at a later line, so a stale position
   320	    claim is unavoidable no matter how much surrounding context is replayed
   321	    with them: the two-line replay, a full-suffix replay and a wholesale
   322	    journal concatenation all fail the same check for the same reason. Identity
   323	    stays in the pair because position alone would accept a truncation that
   324	    happens to realign a different entry onto the recorded line.
   325	
   326	    Physical LINE, never an index among parsed entries: an index is a judgment,
   327	    and a future build that classifies one line differently renumbers every
   328	    entry after it and silently voids every reset bound below.
   329	
   330	    The residual, stated rather than implied: truncating the journal and then
   331	    replaying realigns positions, and this cannot detect it. That is
   332	    journal-rollback territory — the known bound of unsigned local state, the
   333	    same class as deleting the journal outright and being met with
   334	    trust-on-first-use. The named upgrade is the deferred signed grammar, whose
   335	    chained entries make a truncation detectable rather than merely
   336	    disbelieved.
   337	    """
   338	
   339	
   340	
   341	# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   342	# The comparison state machine (§C)
   343	# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   344	
   345	
   346	class Outcome(Enum):
   347	    """The seven rows of the comparison.
   348	
   349	    Seven for six states because §07 lists first contact separately from the
   350	    five comparisons. The outcome *values* are what the conformance vectors
   351	    pin — never a Python exception type, for the reason the replicate family
   352	    gives: naming one implementation's exception family in a language-neutral
   353	    vector pins that family on every other implementation.
   354	    """
   355	
   356	    FIRST_CONTACT = "first-contact"  # proceed, trust on first use, labeled
   357	    UNCHANGED = "unchanged"  # proceed
   358	    ADVANCED = "advanced"  # proceed
   359	    ROLLBACK = "rollback"  # refuse
   360	    SAME_HEIGHT_FORK = "same-height-fork"  # refuse
   361	    REWRITE = "rewrite"  # refuse
   362	    LINEAGE_REPLACED = "lineage-replaced"  # refuse
   363	
   364	
   365	class AbsentStoreOutcome(Enum):
   366	    """The §D.4 split for a location whose log is not there at all.
   367	
   368	    Deliberately a separate enum rather than two more :class:`Outcome` rows.
   369	    :func:`compare` has exactly seven rows and the vectors pin those; an
   370	    absent store is a different question, asked before there is a head to
   371	    present, and folding it in would make "the seven rows" mean nine.
   372	    """
   373	
   374	    PRE_GENESIS = "pre-genesis"  # proceed — this is the sidecar about to mint
   375	    STORE_LOST = "store-lost"  # refuse — the journal remembers what is gone
   376	
   377	
   378	def compare(
   379	    known: HeadAttestation | None,
   380	    presented: Head,
   381	    at_known: Head | None,
   382	) -> Outcome:
   383	    """Judge a presented head against the one this machine remembers.
   384	
   385	    Pure, stdlib-only, no I/O — which is what makes it directly
   386	    vector-testable and what keeps the vectors free of any implementation's
   387	    exception family. This function only judges; the caller gathers.
   388	
   389	    ``at_known`` is the head the ledger vouches for at *K*'s coordinate, or
   390	    ``None`` when it will not vouch for anything there. It is the
   391	    advanced-versus-rewrite discriminator and it is the whole reason a walk
   392	    that merely reaches the presented head is not enough.
   393	
   394	    Guard order is load-bearing. Lineage is checked before any ordinal
   395	    arithmetic, because comparing heights across two different lineages is
   396	    comparing two unrelated number lines. ``at_known`` is consulted only on
   397	    the ascending branch: on the unchanged branch there is nothing to
   398	    discriminate, and asking would make the dominant case pay for evidence it
   399	    does not need.
   400	    """
   401	    if known is None:
   402	        return Outcome.FIRST_CONTACT
   403	
   404	    remembered = known.head
   405	    if presented.lineage != remembered.lineage:
   406	        return Outcome.LINEAGE_REPLACED
   407	    if presented == remembered:
   408	        return Outcome.UNCHANGED
   409	    if presented.ordinal < remembered.ordinal:
   410	        return Outcome.ROLLBACK
   411	    if presented.ordinal == remembered.ordinal:
   412	        # Equal ordinal, and not equal heads, so the hashes differ.
   413	        return Outcome.SAME_HEIGHT_FORK
   414	    if at_known != remembered:
   415	        return Outcome.REWRITE
   416	    return Outcome.ADVANCED
   417	
   418	
   419	def compare_absent_store(known: HeadAttestation | None) -> AbsentStoreOutcome:
   420	    """Judge a location whose log is absent or empty (§D.4).
   421	
   422	    ``verify(Open())`` on a missing or empty log refuses with
   423	    ``GenesisRefused``, so the seam must branch on that rather than treat it
   424	    as corruption — an absent store is the ordinary state of a location a
   425	    moment before it is minted into.
   426	
   427	    The split is on memory, not on the store: with nothing remembered this is
   428	    pre-genesis and refusing would make minting impossible; with a head
   429	    remembered it is a loss, and proceeding would license a silent re-mint
   430	    under a remembered lineage.
   431	    """
   432	    return (
   433	        AbsentStoreOutcome.PRE_GENESIS
   434	        if known is None
   435	        else AbsentStoreOutcome.STORE_LOST
   436	    )
   437	
   438	
   439	#: Which refusal each refusing outcome raises. The mapping lives here so the
   440	#: seam does not re-implement the posture table, and so "which outcomes
   441	#: refuse" is one enumerable fact rather than a chain of ``if`` statements in
   442	#: whatever calls the classifier.
   443	REFUSALS: dict[Outcome, type[AttestationRefusal]] = {
   444	    Outcome.ROLLBACK: HeadRollback,
   445	    Outcome.SAME_HEIGHT_FORK: HeadFork,
   446	    Outcome.REWRITE: HeadRewrite,
   447	    Outcome.LINEAGE_REPLACED: LineageReplaced,
   448	}
   449	
   450	#: The same table for the absent-store split.
   451	ABSENT_STORE_REFUSALS: dict[AbsentStoreOutcome, type[AttestationRefusal]] = {
   452	    AbsentStoreOutcome.STORE_LOST: StoreLost,
   453	}
   454	
   455	
   456	def refusal_for(
   457	    outcome: Outcome | AbsentStoreOutcome,
   458	) -> type[AttestationRefusal] | None:
   459	    """The refusal this outcome raises, or None if it proceeds."""
   460	    if isinstance(outcome, AbsentStoreOutcome):
   461	        return ABSENT_STORE_REFUSALS.get(outcome)
   462	    return REFUSALS.get(outcome)
   463	
   464	
   465	# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   466	# Where the journal lives (§A.3)
   467	# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   468	
   469	#: Conservative: a leading alphanumeric, then alphanumerics, dot, dash,
   470	#: underscore. A ULID passes; a path separator, a leading dot, an empty
   471	#: string, a traversal segment and a name long enough to trouble a filesystem
   472	#: all fail. Narrow by intent — widening it later is additive, and every
   473	#: character admitted is a character some filesystem treats specially.
   474	_SAFE_LINEAGE = re.compile(r"\A[A-Za-z0-9][A-Za-z0-9._-]{0,127}\Z")
   475	
   476	#: Names the heads directory already spends on something else. ``bindings``
   477	#: is the transitional binding file below, and a lineage literally named
   478	#: "bindings" would otherwise write its observations into it. Compared
   479	#: case-folded because the filesystems this runs on are commonly
   480	#: case-insensitive, so ``BINDINGS`` collides just as hard.
   481	_RESERVED_LINEAGE_NAMES = frozenset({"bindings"})
   482	
   483	#: The journal grammar version. A literal rather than an import of
   484	#: ``engine.arrival.GRAMMAR_VERSION``: reaching for it would drag the whole
   485	#: file codec into a module whose readability must not depend on it, and the
   486	#: two version each other independently anyway — this is the journal's
   487	#: grammar, not the record grammar.
   488	_JOURNAL_VERSION = 1
   489	
   490	#: Written into the header so a journal's hash derivation is RECORDED. §B.3
   491	#: asks for more than that — that a v1 journal never be silently compared
   492	#: against a v2 head whose hashes derive differently — and nothing reads these
   493	#: back yet, so the promise is only half kept. Stated rather than implied,
   494	#: because a comment claiming the enforcement would be the same stale-prose
   495	#: defect this file has already paid for three times. The forcing consumer is
   496	#: wire v2; sol r1 made it concrete, since a v2-shaped header is now
   497	#: recognized as a header and its version ignored.
   498	_PROTOCOL_VERSION = 1
   499	_WIRE_VERSION = 1
   500	
   501	#: The serialized ``type``. Never bare "attestation" — see the module
   502	#: docstring's vocabulary note.
   503	_OBSERVATION_TYPE = "arrival-head-observation"
   504	
   505	
   506	def state_root() -> Path:
   507	    """The per-user state root for loops, honoring ``XDG_STATE_HOME``.
   508	
   509	    A *state* root, not a config root, and that separation is the whole
   510	    threat-model argument: the live config-level stores are themselves under
   511	    ``$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/loops``, so a memory kept there would share a restore
   512	    boundary with the very stores it witnesses. Same for a sibling file beside
   513	    the store, for ``.loops/state/``, and for git tracking as a sole home. A
   514	    state root is outside all of them.
   515	
   516	    Follows ``vertex_reader._loops_home()``'s idiom for the config root, with
   517	    the standard ``~/.local/state`` fallback, so tests can point it at a
   518	    temporary directory the way the CLI tests already do for config.
   519	    """
   520	    xdg = os.environ.get("XDG_STATE_HOME", str(Path.home() / ".local" / "state"))
  1280	    text = (repo_root / source).read_text()
  1281	    assert "DELETE IN SLICE 5" in text
  1282	    assert "residue sweep" in text
  1283	
