   717	    Whatever the read missed carries no information about the ordinal it
   718	    held, so the readable entries bound the accepted head from below and
   719	    nothing bounds it from above. Deliberately **no** ``entry`` attribute: the
   720	    field is named ``at_least`` so that no expression reaches a head from this
   721	    type without naming the weaker claim, and so a caller cannot reach one
   722	    uniformly across both cases.
   723	
   724	    What a bound can and cannot answer:
   725	
   726	    * **Sound.** A presented head *below* the bound is a rollback — the
   727	      accepted head is at least the bound, so it is certainly above what is
   728	      being presented.
   729	    * **Unsound, and this is the trap.** A presented head *equal to* the bound
   730	      is not unchanged; it may be a rollback from the very entry the read
   731	      missed.
   732	    * **Unsound for the same reason, one step further.** A presented head
   733	      *above* the bound is not an advance either. Nothing bounds the accepted
   734	      head from above, so a head at ordinal 95 may still sit below a lost
   735	      entry at 200 — and descent verified from the bound says nothing about
   736	      an entry that was never on the walk. Both proceed answers are
   737	      unobtainable; only the refusal below the bound survives.
