  1199	        refusal demanding an operator decision, or proceed under an explicitly
  1200	        labeled degradation? **It proceeds — after firing every refusal the
  1201	        incomplete read still soundly supports.** That is not the two-valued
  1202	        question it looks like, and the third answer is the whole of it.
  1203	
  1204	        **Why not refuse.** An incomplete read persists until an operator
  1205	        acts. The journal is append-only and never rewritten, and the torn-
  1206	        tail guard preserves a crashed writer's fragment rather than removing
  1207	        it, so a line lost to an ordinary crash sits in ``skipped`` on every
  1208	        later read — recorded forever, weighed only until a trust-reset
  1209	        ceremony decrees past it, since the epoch-scoped weighting stops
  1210	        counting a loss the decree superseded. Until that ceremony, a seam
  1211	        that refused on it would make one crashed writer a store that never
  1212	        opens again — which is verbatim the argument WP1's amended ruling used
  1213	        to REJECT refusing on mid-file damage, one level down: "it made an
  1214	        ordinary crash a permanent incident, and it claimed a protection this
  1215	        location cannot deliver anyway — anyone able to corrupt the journal can
  1216	        delete it instead and be met with trust-on-first-use." Refusing here
  1217	        would re-impose at the composition boundary the exact verdict the
  1218	        module removed, which is WP1's own write-path lesson generalized: a
  1219	        rule relaxed in a module can be re-tightened by its caller, undoing the
  1220	        ruling without touching the code it was made about.
  1221	
  1222	        **Why "proceed" alone would be dishonest, and what makes it not that.**
  1223	        A bound is not nothing. Two of the four refusals are still SOUND
  1224	        against it and both are raised here:
  1225	
  1226	        * A presented head BELOW the bound is a rollback. The accepted head is
  1227	          at least the bound, so it is certainly above what is being presented.
  1228	        * A presented head at the bound's ordinal with a DIFFERENT hash is a
  1229	          same-height fork. The bound is a real entry — a head this machine
  1230	          accepted at that ordinal — and the store presents a different record
  1231	          there. The lost lines cannot make that agree.
  1232	
  1233	        What remains indeterminate is the region at and above the bound:
  1234	        equal is not unchanged (it may be a rollback from the very entry the
