   392	          computation—because a recovery path that depended on the read it recovers from would
   393	          be unusable at exactly the moment it is needed, which is a journal already
   394	          equivocating. The position claim then buys its protection with an obligation: there is
   395	          no lock across read-tail-then-append, so an automated append landing in that window
   396	          leaves a freshly written, legitimate reset carrying a position that is already stale.
   397	          The ceremony therefore reads its own entry back and raises
   398	          <code>TrustResetNotHonored</code> when it did not take effect. Nothing is lost and
   399	          nothing is wrongly accepted in that case—the previous head still stands and opens keep
   400	          refusing—but an operator who believes their store will now open is holding a false
   401	          belief the next open would contradict, and silence is the only unsafe part.
   402	        </p>
   403	        <p>
   404	          <strong>What a reset does and does not fence out</strong>, stated because it is
   405	          underdetermined rather than decided: a reset decrees trust in <em>N</em>; verified
   406	          descendants of <em>N</em>, including re-presentations of abandoned history, are
   407	          accepted; fencing out authentic history is not expressible unsigned. The ceremony’s
   408	          semantics here remain an open ruling
   409	          (<code>design:arrival-reset-descendant-acceptance</code>).
   410	        </p>
   411	        <p>
   412	          The residual, stated rather than implied: truncating the journal and then replaying
   413	          realigns every position, and nothing here detects it. That is the known bound of
   414	          unsigned local state—the same class as deleting the journal outright and being met
   415	          with first contact—and §04’s chained signed entries are the named upgrade.
   416	        </p>
   417	
   418	        <h3>A repeated line is a re-assertion</h3>
   419	        <p>
   420	          A line whose stripped text duplicates an earlier line in the same file is skipped and
   421	          recorded <em>before</em> the head is computed: it re-asserts an observation the
   422	          journal already holds rather than recording a new one, and a replayed line cannot
   423	          count twice. The rule is type-agnostic—advances, audits, and resets pass one gate—and
   424	          order-agnostic, so it survives the non-ascending write order §07 describes. It is
   425	          read-side only: no record shape, producer, or locking changes. Header lines never enter
   426	          it, which is why the create-race duplicate above is tolerated rather than deduplicated.
   427	        </p>
   428	        <p>
   429	          The record <strong>carries no weight</strong> (§07): its content is a line this read
   430	          already counted, so nothing about the accepted head is unknown because of it, and a
   431	          journal whose only losses are re-assertions still reads as established.
   432	        </p>
   433	
   434	        <div class="doc-callout is-security">
   435	          <span class="doc-callout-label">Why bytes, and why before <em>K</em></span>
   436	          <p>
   437	            <em>K</em> is the maximum ordinal of the current epoch, so a replayed historical
   438	            advance would be taken as current and would name a head an operator had deliberately
   439	            abandoned. A store restored from the pre-reset backup then presents exactly that
   440	            head, and “unchanged” carries no descent check by design—so ceremonially abandoned
   441	            state would be re-accepted in silence. Literal replay of existing bytes is the
   442	            unsigned threat model, and byte identity is complete against it. Because the skip
   443	            carries no weight, the match predicate is acceptance-load-bearing, which is worth
   444	            saying plainly: a false match discards a real line, lowers <em>K</em>, and still
   445	            reads as established. What holds the line there is that an advance demands a
   446	            verified descendant rather than mere height.
   447	          </p>
   448	        </div>
   449	
   450	        <h3>First contact and the object behind a name</h3>
   451	        <p>
   452	          A location is bound to its lineage when first witnessed, and the binding key is a
   453	          resolved path—relative segments made absolute, <code>..</code> collapsed, symlinks
   454	          followed—so that two vertices reaching one log by different routes present one key.
   455	          That key is a <strong>spelling, not an identity</strong>. Resolving a path normalizes
   456	          it; it does not establish which filesystem object the path names. A case-variant
   457	          spelling on a case-insensitive filesystem, or a second mount of the same volume,
   458	          reaches the very same store through a string that resolution leaves untouched and
   459	          therefore still distinct from the one already bound.
   460	        </p>
   461	        <p>
   462	          So before a presenting location with no binding of its own is minted as first contact,
   463	          each recorded binding is live-stat’d and its device and inode compared. A match routes
   464	          the open through the existing binding, where a replacement bearing a fresh lineage
   465	          meets a lineage-replaced refusal instead of walking past it. That identity is compared
   466	          live and never recorded: inodes go stale when a file is recreated and are recycled
   467	          onto unrelated files, so a stored one is a claim that rots into a false match. Asking
   468	          the filesystem which object a name currently denotes is legitimate here because the
   469	          filesystem namespace is ambient authority—two names for one object is a property of
   470	          the namespace, not of the store—and the answer is a location claim about this host
   570	
   571	      <section class="doc-section" id="comparison">
   572	        <div class="doc-section-header">
   573	          <span class="doc-section-number">07</span>
   574	          <h2>Head comparison state machine</h2>
   575	        </div>
   576	        <p>
   577	          Let <em>K</em> be the head this verifier already accepted and <em>P</em> the head now
   578	          presented by the authority. Comparison uses lineage, ordinal, and record hash—never
   579	          timestamp alone.
   580	        </p>
   581	        <p>
   582	          <em>K</em> is the <strong>maximum-ordinal entry of the current trust epoch</strong>,
   583	          and both halves of that are load-bearing. Not the last entry written: appends are
   584	          serialized, journal writes are not, so two writers can leave a file holding ordinals
   585	          90, 92, 91 in that order. Last-write semantics would read <em>K</em> = 91 there and
   586	          call a genuine restore to 91 unchanged. Duplicate entries at the maximum are harmless;
   587	          two entries at the maximum with <em>different</em> record hashes are equivocation, and
   588	          the journal has recorded both rather than choosing between them.
   589	        </p>
   590	        <p>
   591	          Not unscoped, either. A trust epoch is the last honored trust-reset entry and
   592	          everything after it, <strong>inclusive of the reset entry itself</strong>, which
   593	          carries the head the operator accepted—scoping it out would leave a just-reset journal
   594	          with an empty epoch and a next open classifying first contact. Omitting the epoch
   595	          altogether is the mirror deadlock: after an operator accepts a restore from ordinal
   596	          100 back to 90, the abandoned entries at 91–100 still hold the maximum ordinal, so an
   597	          unscoped <em>K</em> would name the head that was deliberately abandoned and every
   598	          later open would refuse a rollback forever. Those entries stay in the journal as
   599	          <strong>evidence, not as claims</strong>. Equivocation is scoped the same way, so a
   600	          ceremony can take effect over a conflict the operator has already resolved.
   601	        </p>
   602	
   603	        <h3>How much of a head the journal has</h3>
   604	        <p>
   605	          <em>K</em> is not head-or-nothing. Reading the journal (§04b) yields one of four
   606	          states, and the weaker ones must not be collapsed into the others: a journal holding
   607	          content none of which can be read says something quite different from an empty one,
   608	          and trusting a store on first use <em>because</em> its journal became unreadable is
   609	          the silent re-acceptance this protocol exists to prevent.
   610	        </p>
   611	
   612	        <div class="doc-table-wrap">
   613	          <table class="doc-spec-table">
   614	            <thead>
   615	              <tr><th>Read state</th><th>When</th><th>What it supports</th></tr>
   616	            </thead>
   617	            <tbody>
   618	              <tr><td>Established head</td><td>Nothing the read missed carried weight</td><td>All seven comparisons below.</td></tr>
   619	              <tr><td>Head lower bound—<em>at least</em> this</td><td>The read missed something that carried weight</td><td>Rollback below the bound; same-height fork at the bound with a differing hash. Equal is not unchanged and higher is not advanced.</td></tr>
   620	              <tr><td>Head unreadable</td><td>Content was claimed and none of it could be read</td><td>Nothing—there is no bound to compare against, rollback included. Not first contact: heads <em>were</em> accepted here.</td></tr>
   621	              <tr><td>No head</td><td>The journal is absent, or holds only a header</td><td>First contact.</td></tr>
   622	            </tbody>
   623	          </table>
   624	        </div>
   625	
   626	        <p>
   627	          A read is <strong>incomplete</strong> when it lacked something it needed <em>and</em>
   628	          that loss carried weight—a line that did not parse, a line that could not be
   629	          classified, an entry written by a later build, a voided reset, or the header. The term
   630	          names what the read lacked rather than why, so a new cause joins it without new
   631	          vocabulary. “Incomplete,” never “torn”—damage at the end of the file is not a
   632	          privileged case. Because journal writes do not ascend in file order, damage anywhere
   633	          can take the epoch maximum, so a weighing loss mid-file yields a lower bound by
   634	          construction rather than by concession.
   635	        </p>
   636	        <p>
   637	          Nothing bounds the accepted head from <em>above</em>, and that asymmetry is the trap.
   638	          A presented head equal to the bound may be a rollback from the very entry the read
   639	          missed; one above the bound may still sit below a lost entry higher still, and descent
   640	          verified from the bound says nothing about an entry that was never on the walk.
   641	          Gathering more evidence from the store repairs neither, because the missing fact is
   642	          about what this machine previously <em>accepted</em>, which the store never knew.
   643	          Recovering it is operator work: repair the journal, or run the trust-reset ceremony.
   644	        </p>
   645	
   646	        <h3>Which skips weaken: weight is cause times position</h3>
   647	        <p>
   648	          A recorded skip and a weakening skip are not the same thing. The bound exists to
   649	          represent <em>ignorance</em>, so a skip that leaves the reader ignorant of nothing must
   650	          not produce one. Weight is the product of two factors, and both are decided
   651	          <strong>per skip, never per read</strong>: a journal holding one weightless skip and
   652	          one weighing skip is bounded, by the second alone.
   653	        </p>
   654	        <p>
   655	          <strong>Cause.</strong> A re-assertion (§04b) carries zero weight—the skipped line’s
   656	          content is a line this read already counted, so nothing about the accepted head is
   657	          unknown because of it. Every other cause weighs in full. Weighing a re-assertion would
   658	          let one benign crash-retry duplicate bound every read of an append-only file for the
   659	          life of that file, which is the ordinary-crash-becomes-permanent-incident shape these
   660	          rules keep resolving against. A <em>voided reset</em> does weigh, and the distinction
   661	          is worth stating: its line is perfectly readable, but what the operator intended by it
   662	          is not, and that intent uncertainty is real ignorance.
   663	        </p>
   664	        <p>
   665	          <strong>Position.</strong> A weakening-class skip sitting at a physical line
   666	          <em>before the boundary reset’s line</em> carries zero weight. The ceremony decreed
   667	          trust in a head, and everything positionally behind that decree is what it decreed
   668	          past; at or after the boundary, the cause weight applies in full. Without this the
   669	          ceremony heals nothing—an operator resets past a damaged line and still gets a bounded
   670	          read forever, from the very damage the reset was run to put behind them.
   671	        </p>
   672	
   673	        <div class="doc-callout is-warning">
   674	          <span class="doc-callout-label">Position is not ordinal</span>
   675	          <p>
   676	            The coordinate here is the physical line, the same one a reset’s binding uses, and
   677	            ordinals never enter it. That is the exact opposite of the rule stated above for
   678	            <em>K</em>, and both are true for one reason: ordinals do not ascend in file order
   679	            because two writers interleave them, while positions cannot interleave, because the
   680	            filesystem serializes appends. Appends are tail-only, so a line’s position in the
   681	            file <em>is</em> its place in time.
   682	          </p>
   683	        </div>
   684	
   685	        <p>
   686	          Three further edges, each a case the rule would otherwise get wrong. A
   687	          <strong>structural absence is exempt from position scoping</strong>: a missing header
   688	          weighs regardless of any boundary, because the header would be line 1 and sit below
   689	          every boundary, yet its absence is a claim about the file rather than a line an
   690	          operator decreed past—which is also why it is reported without a line number.
   691	          <strong>No valid reset means no boundary</strong>, and then every weakening cause keeps
   692	          full weight; “there is nothing to be after” is a different situation from “this is
   693	          after it.” And a bound that survives all this is <strong>epoch-scoped like <em>K</em>
   694	          itself</strong>—a file-wide bound inflated by an abandoned epoch’s high ordinal would
   695	          manufacture rollback refusals against genuine current-epoch stores, which is wrong even
   696	          though it errs on the refusal side.
   697	        </p>
   698	        <p>
   699	          <strong>Two mechanisms heal, not one</strong>, and they cover different damage.
   700	          Positioned damage heals through the weighting above. A ceremony attempt voided by the
   701	          race described in §04b heals through the epoch walk instead: the walk runs backward and
   702	          returns at the first valid reset it meets, so a re-run ceremony’s own entry ends the
   703	          walk before the failed attempt below it is ever examined, and no note is produced for
   704	          it at all. The corollary is that any voided-reset note a read <em>does</em> carry comes
   705	          from above the boundary by construction, which is why position scoping never exempts
   706	          one. Unhealed degradation is honest; healed degradation is decreed.
   707	        </p>
   708	        <p>
   709	          The distinction is executable rather than prose: the conformance vectors count recorded
   710	          skips and weighing skips separately (<code>skipped</code> and <code>weakening</code> in
   711	          <code>spec/conformance/SCHEMA.md</code> §11), so an implementation cannot satisfy them
   712	          while collapsing the two.
   713	        </p>
   714	
   715	        <div class="doc-callout is-security">
   716	          <span class="doc-callout-label">Degrade with a label; do not refuse</span>
   717	          <p>
   718	            An incomplete read persists until an operator acts—the journal is append-only, so a
   719	            lost line stays lost and keeps its weight until a ceremony decrees past it—and
   720	            refusing on one would therefore make a single crashed writer a store that never opens
   721	            again without intervention. It would also buy nothing against an adversary, who can
   722	            delete the journal outright and be met with first contact. An open against a bound therefore fires
   723	            every refusal the bound still soundly supports and then proceeds under an explicitly
   724	            labeled degradation that claims no comparison outcome at all. It writes nothing—no
   725	            journal entry, no binding—so the degradation never launders itself into a memory and
   726	            the bound never rises on evidence the read did not have.
   727	          </p>
   728	        </div>
   729	
   730	        <h3>The seven comparisons</h3>
   731	        <p>
   732	          Sound against an established head. Guard order is normative: lineage is judged before
   733	          any ordinal arithmetic, because heights in two different lineages are unrelated number
   734	          lines.
   735	        </p>
   785	          A verifier with no cached head or pinned witness cannot distinguish the authentic
   786	          lineage from a carefully constructed replacement. Migration should therefore emit a
   787	          bootstrap receipt, and new devices should retrieve a checkpoint from an already trusted
   788	          device or witness rather than trusting only the database they are meant to audit.
   789	        </p>
   790	        <p>
   791	          First contact is also a state a verifier can reach by accident, through a store it has
   792	          in fact seen before under another spelling of the same location. §04b covers the
   793	          identity check that closes that route before a first contact is minted.
   794	        </p>
   795	      </section>
   796	
   797	      <section class="doc-section" id="attacks">
   798	        <div class="doc-section-header">
   799	          <span class="doc-section-number">08</span>
   800	          <h2>Attack and failure matrix</h2>
   801	        </div>
   802	
   803	        <div class="doc-table-wrap">
   804	          <table class="doc-spec-table">
   805	            <thead>
   806	              <tr><th>Event</th><th>Local evidence</th><th>External evidence</th><th>Result</th></tr>
   807	            </thead>
   808	            <tbody>
   809	              <tr><td>Flip a byte in an interior body</td><td><code>rh</code> fails, then chain breaks</td><td>Known head remains different</td><td>Immediate corruption refusal</td></tr>
   810	              <tr><td>Delete an interior record only</td><td>Ordinal gap and predecessor mismatch</td><td>Known head corroborates old prefix</td><td>Immediate corruption refusal</td></tr>
   811	              <tr><td>Delete a projection row</td><td>Ledger verifies; projection audit disagrees</td><td>Head unchanged</td><td>Rebuild projection</td></tr>
   812	              <tr><td>Delete the last ten records</td><td>Short prefix self-verifies</td><td>Presented ordinal is lower</td><td>Rollback refusal</td></tr>
   813	              <tr><td>Restore an old database backup</td><td>Old prefix self-verifies</td><td>Known head is newer</td><td>Rollback refusal</td></tr>
   814	              <tr><td>Edit and rehash all later records</td><td>Forged chain self-verifies</td><td>Same or later height does not descend from known hash</td><td>Fork / rewrite refusal</td></tr>
   815	              <tr><td>Rewrite head metadata only</td><td>Metadata disagrees with actual head record or dense scan</td><td>Known head assists diagnosis</td><td>Metadata corruption refusal</td></tr>
   816	              <tr><td>Show two clients different descendants</td><td>Each branch may self-verify</td><td>Receipt gossip reveals incompatible hashes</td><td>Equivocation evidence</td></tr>
   817	              <tr><td>Replay a journal line verbatim</td><td>Store untouched</td><td>Bytes already appear above it: a re-assertion, recorded and weightless (§04b)</td><td>Remembered head unmoved</td></tr>
   818	              <tr><td>Re-append an old trust reset to reopen a closed epoch</td><td>Store may present the abandoned head</td><td>A byte copy is a re-assertion; any copy lands at a new line and its recorded position is stale (§04b)</td><td>Reset opens no epoch; rollback refusal stands</td></tr>
   819	              <tr><td>Delete store and every backup</td><td>No history remains</td><td>Witness proves what was lost, not its contents</td><td>Detectable loss, not recovery</td></tr>
   820	              <tr><td>Compromise backend and sole witness key</td><td>Replacement can self-verify</td><td>Attacker can forge receipts</td><td>Outside claimed protection</td></tr>
   821	            </tbody>
   822	          </table>
   823	        </div>
   824	
   825	        <div class="doc-callout">
   826	          <span class="doc-callout-label">The direct answer</span>
   827	          <p>
   828	            Yes: a manually deleted canonical row computes differently. Interior deletion is
   829	            caught by density and chain verification; tail deletion is caught by comparing the
   830	            now-shorter head to the last externally remembered head. If the administrator also
   831	            rehashes the remaining history, that new head still differs from the remembered one.
   832	          </p>
   833	        </div>
   834	      </section>
